INT. SUBURBAN KITCHEN - NIGHT

Dark. A power cut. One camping lantern on the island. A handheld radio crackles beside it.

THE WOMAN (40s, wet coat) stands in the doorway. ASH (30s) holds a kitchen knife, shaking.

ASH
You came through the back door. The back door has been locked since Tuesday. Tell me how that happens. Tell me, and use small words, because I have been awake since the sky went green, and they are tired.

THE WOMAN
(beat)
I didn't open it. It was already open.

RADIO (V.O.)
(static)
Ash? Ash, do you copy? The grid's not coming back. Repeat, not coming back.

ASH
(not taking their eyes off her)
Copy, Mum. Stay in the cellar.

THE WOMAN
(beat)
I'm not here to hurt anyone. My name is Delphine. I was in the lab when it started. I know what's in the sky, and I know why the lights went. I have forty minutes of explanation and you have, what, a bread knife?

ASH
(lowering it an inch)
It's a good bread knife.

DELPHINE
(a short laugh, then not)
I believe you.

The lantern flickers. Out. On.

RADIO (V.O.)
Ash. There's someone at the window.

Ash looks. Delphine looks. Nothing but black glass.

ASH
(whispering)
Tell me everything. Start with what you did.
